      "topics": [
        {
          "id": "labels", "title": "Value labels", "summary": "Each value written beside its bar or point, on any series, past a bar's end or in the middle of a stacked segment, with labels that would overlap left out.", "members": ["ChartBarSeries.Labels", "ChartBarSeries.LabelFormat", "ChartBarSeries.LabelOverlap"],
          "examples": [
            { "id": "chart-labels", "title": "Labels on bars", "lead": "To write each value beside its bar, set `Labels` on the series to `Auto`. A label goes past the end of the bar, or inside it when there's no room above.", "after": ["`LabelFormat` formats the labels apart from the axis. A label that would overlap another is left out at the widths where it would; narrow the window to see them go. To show every label anyway, set `LabelOverlap` to `Show`.", "With every series labeled, the chart has no tooltips, which would repeat the labels. To keep them, set `Tooltip` to true."] },
            { "id": "chart-labels-stacked", "title": "Labels in a stack", "lead": "In a stack, `Auto` puts each label in the middle of its segment, and leaves it out when the segment is too small to hold it. `Inside` puts labels inside the bar at its end, and `Outside` past it." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "reference", "title": "Reference lines and notes", "summary": "Lines at a target or an average, shaded ranges such as a season, and notes at a point, each labeled in text and said in the chart's description.", "members": ["ChartReferenceLine", "ChartReferenceBand", "ChartAnnotation"],
          "examples": [
            { "id": "chart-reference", "title": "A target, an average and a season", "lead": "To mark a value, such as a target, put a `ChartReferenceLine` in the chart with its series and set its `Value`. It's a dashed line with its `Label` at the line's end, or where `LabelAlign` puts it: `Start`, `Center` or `End`.", "after": ["The value axis always reaches a reference line, so a target above every value still shows. With `Statistic` and a series' name as `Series`, the line is that series' `Average` or `Median`.", "`ChartReferenceBand` shades a range of values from `From` to `To`, or of arguments from `ArgumentFrom` to `ArgumentTo`, here November and December."] },
            { "id": "chart-annotations", "title": "Notes on a line", "lead": "`ChartAnnotation` puts a short `Text` at an `Argument` and `Value`, with a line down to the point. A `ChartReferenceLine` with an `Argument` in place of a `Value` draws a line at an argument, here the day of a launch. Hiding a series from the legend rescales the chart, and the marks move with it." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "trends", "title": "Trends", "summary": "A dashed line through any series' values: the straight line or curve that fits them best, or a moving average that smooths out the ups and downs.", "members": ["ChartBarSeries.Trend", "ChartBarSeries.TrendOrder", "ChartBarSeries.TrendPeriod", "ChartBarSeries.TrendColor"],
          "examples": [
            { "id": "chart-trend", "title": "A linear trend", "lead": "To draw a series' trend as a dashed line, set `Trend` on the series: `Linear`, the straight line that fits best, or `Polynomial`, a curve of `TrendOrder` (2, a parabola, by default).", "after": ["The trend is named after the series, here \"Revenue trend\", in the legend, the tooltip and Show data. The legend hides it like any series.", "It's drawn in the series' color mixed toward the text color, so it reads over the series' own bars. `TrendColor` sets a color of your own."] },
            { "id": "chart-moving-average", "title": "A moving average", "lead": "`MovingAverage` makes each point the average of the last `TrendPeriod` values, here 7 days, which smooths out the weekend dips. It starts once the first window is full." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "second-axis", "title": "A second axis", "summary": "A second value axis across the plot from the first, for a measure in other units read against the same arguments, with ticks on the first axis's grid lines.", "members": ["ChartValueAxis.Name", "ChartLineSeries.ValueAxis"],
          "examples": [
            { "id": "chart-second-axis", "title": "Revenue and margin", "lead": "To plot a series in other units, such as a margin read against its revenue, add a second `ChartValueAxis` with a `Name`, here `margin`. Then give that series the name as its `ValueAxis`.", "after": ["Two charts side by side usually read better than one with two axes: readers compare the lines' heights, which mean nothing across scales. Use a second axis when one chart is the better fit.", "The second axis has its own `Format`, `Min`, `Max` and `Title`. Its ticks fall on the first axis's grid lines, each a round step. Each title has a key in its series' color.", "A second axis needs an upright chart, and a stacked chart's bars and areas stay on one axis. Reference lines, bands and notes are read on the second axis with the same `ValueAxis`; a statistic line follows its series."] }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "axes", "title": "Axes", "summary": "Titles beside the axes, a set gap between ticks, a log scale, grid lines, values on either side, and names that turn or wrap when they crowd.", "members": ["ChartValueAxis", "ChartArgumentAxis", "ChartValueAxis.Position"],
          "examples": [
            { "id": "chart-axes", "title": "Titles, a set step and grid lines", "lead": "`ChartValueAxis` and `ChartArgumentAxis` set up each axis. `Title` puts a title beside it, and the value axis's `Step` sets the gap between its ticks, here $25,000.", "after": ["The value axis ends on a multiple of its step. The argument axis's title also heads the first column of the data table.", "`GridLines` on the argument axis adds lines between the months; on the value axis, `GridLines=\"false\"` leaves only the zero line."] },
            { "id": "chart-log-scale", "title": "A log scale", "lead": "For values that span several powers of ten, set `Logarithmic` on the `ChartValueAxis`: each power of ten is an equal step up, so growth from hundreds to thousands shows as clearly as from hundreds of thousands to millions. A log scale has no zero, so values at or under zero aren't drawn." },
            { "id": "chart-rotated", "title": "Long names along the bottom", "lead": "Names that don't fit side by side are skipped, every other one first. To turn them 45° before any are skipped, set the `ChartArgumentAxis`'s `LabelFit` to `Rotate`; narrow the window to see them turn. `Wrap` shows every name, wrapped onto more lines in its band, which suits a few long names." },
            { "id": "chart-formatters", "title": "Your own label text", "lead": "When a format string can't say it, an axis's `Formatter` writes its text. Here the `ChartValueAxis` writes revenue in millions, and the `ChartArgumentAxis` names each quarter from its start date.", "after": ["The text shows on the axis, in tooltips and in the data table. Hiding a series from the legend then keeps the value axis as it is, since the browser can't run your formatter.", "`HideLabels` leaves an axis's labels out. `--ib-chart-tick-color` and `--ib-chart-tick-size` set the labels' color and size."] },
            { "id": "chart-reversed", "title": "Arguments right to left", "lead": "To run the arguments right to left, set `Reverse` on `ChartArgumentAxis`, here January at the right and December at the left. `Position=\"ChartAxisPosition.Right\"` on the `ChartValueAxis` moves the values to the right, beside January, where the reading starts.", "after": ["On a right-to-left page the plot keeps its direction, as most charts in Arabic and Hebrew apps do; the legend, title and tooltip follow the page. `Reverse` works on its own, with no `dir` set.", "With the values on the right, a second axis takes the left. On a horizontal chart, `Reverse` puts the first item at the bottom, and `Top` puts the values above the bars."] }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "colors", "title": "Colors", "summary": "Series take the theme's eight colors, shades of one color for series in an order, two colors either side of a middle, or your own.", "members": ["Palette", "ChartBarSeries.Color"],
          "examples": [
            { "id": "chart-shades", "title": "Shades for tiers", "lead": "When the series are in an order, such as plan tiers, set `Palette` to `ChartPalette.Shades` with one color, here the theme's primary. The first series is the lightest and each one after is darker.", "after": ["In dark mode the ramp turns round, so the last series is the brightest. The shades are spread over as many series as the chart has. Past about six they're hard to tell apart; for series in no order, keep the default `ChartPalette.Colors`."] },
            { "id": "chart-diverging", "title": "A survey from disagree to agree", "lead": "For series on either side of a middle, such as answers from disagree to agree, set `Palette` to `ChartPalette.Diverging` with the two ends' colors.", "after": ["The first series is the strongest of the first color and the last the strongest of the second. With an odd count, the middle one is gray.", "`ChartPalette.Of` takes your own colors in order, and a series' `Color` still sets that one series."] }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "tooltips", "title": "Tooltips and the legend", "summary": "Each series' value for the item under the pointer or keyboard focus, from a template if you give one, and a legend above, below or beside the chart.", "members": ["Tooltip", "ChartTooltip", "Legend", "LegendAlign", "TitleAlign", "ChartBarSeries.Hidden", "DataTable"],
          "examples": [
            { "id": "chart-tooltips", "title": "A tooltip template and a legend", "lead": "To write the tooltip yourself, put a `ChartTooltip` in the chart. Its context has the `Item`, the formatted `Argument`, and each series' `Points` with their names, values and colors.", "after": ["To show the tooltips from the keyboard, Tab to the chart and use the arrow keys. A screen reader hears each value as they move.", "The tooltip is rendered on the server with the page, so keep the template to text and inline content. To leave the tooltips out, set `Tooltip` to false. They're off by default when every series has value labels.", "Click a legend item to hide its series, and again to show it. The chart is drawn again without it, so stacks close up and the value axis rescales. To start a series hidden, set `Hidden`.", "`Legend` places the legend: `Top`, `Bottom`, `Start`, `End` or `None`. `Start` and `End` put it beside the chart, and above or below it under 30rem wide. By default it's above, under the title, with two series or more.", "`LegendAlign` sets where the legend sits along its edge: `Start` (the default, lined up with the title), `Center` or `End`. Above or below the chart, that's left, center or right; beside it, top, middle or bottom. `TitleAlign` moves the title the same way."], "notes": {"noscript": "There are no tooltips, and the legend doesn't hide series. Each bar and marker still shows its value on hover, and Show data lists every value."} }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "bar-chart", "icon": "ChartColumn", "name": "ChartBarSeries", "title": "Bar chart", "category": "Charts", "summary": "Compares values across names as bars: ranked across, grouped or stacked as columns.", "keywords": ["bar chart", "column chart", "bar graph", "stacked bar chart", "grouped bar chart"],
      "features": [
        { "feature": "Bars across, or upright columns" },
        { "feature": "Grouped side by side, stacked, or stacked to 100%" },
        { "feature": "Drawn on the server, as wide as its container, with labels that thin out as it narrows" },
        { "feature": "A table of the values in a \"Show data\" disclosure" },
        { "feature": "Tooltips on hover and from the keyboard", "noscript": "none" },
        { "feature": "Legend items that hide and show a series", "noscript": "none" }
      ],
      "examples": [
        { "id": "chart-horizontal", "title": "Ranked bars across", "lead": "`ChartBarSeries` in a `Chart` draws a bar for each item's `Value`. Ranked names read best as bars across, longest first: set `Orientation` to `Horizontal`.", "after": ["Long names have room down the side, ending in an ellipsis past a third of the chart's width.", "With `Labels` writing each value at its bar's end, the value axis's labels and grid lines only repeat them. Here `HideLabels` and `GridLines=\"false\"` on `ChartValueAxis` leave them out.", "Everything on the Chart page works with every series type."] },
        { "id": "chart-grouped", "title": "Grouped columns", "lead": "Two or more `ChartBarSeries` stand side by side in each band, to compare the series name by name. To compare this year with last, draw last year first and in gray with `Color`, so the current year stands out in the chart's first color." },
        { "id": "chart-stacked", "title": "Stacked", "lead": "`Stacking=\"ChartStacking.Normal\"` stacks the series, so each column's height is the total and each segment one part of it. The bottom segment reads most exactly, since it alone starts at zero: put the largest or most important part first. Keep to a few parts." },
        { "id": "chart-stacked-percent", "title": "Shares of 100%", "lead": "`Stacking=\"ChartStacking.Percent\"` draws each stack to 100%, to compare how a whole divides rather than how big it is. As bars across, each runs the full width and the shares read left to right." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "line-chart", "icon": "ChartLine", "name": "ChartLineSeries", "title": "Line chart", "category": "Charts", "summary": "Shows how values change over time as lines, on a date or number scale.", "keywords": ["line chart", "line graph", "time series", "trend chart"],
      "features": [
        { "feature": "Lines over dates, numbers or names, straight, smooth or stepped" },
        { "feature": "Dashes and marker shapes that tell lines apart besides color" },
        { "feature": "Each line's name at its end, nudged apart where lines end close together" },
        { "feature": "Drawn on the server, as wide as its container, with labels that thin out as it narrows" },
        { "feature": "A table of the values in a \"Show data\" disclosure" },
        { "feature": "Tooltips on hover and from the keyboard", "noscript": "none" },
        { "feature": "Legend items that hide and show a series", "noscript": "none" }
      ],
      "examples": [
        { "id": "chart-line-usage", "title": "A few lines over time", "lead": "`ChartLineSeries` in a `Chart` draws each item's `Value` as a point on a line. To write each line's name where it ends, so the reader doesn't look across to a legend, set `EndLabel`.", "after": ["With dates as the `Argument`, the points sit on a date scale, with a tick per month or year as the width allows.", "Keep to a few lines. Each takes the next color and a dash pattern and marker of its own, so they differ by more than color. With every line named, the chart leaves the legend out.", "Everything on the Chart page works with every series type."] },
        { "id": "chart-lines", "title": "Daily values and a moving average", "lead": "Daily numbers swing with the day of the week, which hides where they're heading. `Trend=\"ChartTrend.MovingAverage\"` with a `TrendPeriod` of 7 draws the average of each week beside the daily line, so the direction reads at a glance and the days stay in view. With this many points, the line has no markers." },
        { "id": "chart-line-smooth", "title": "Smooth lines", "lead": "`Curve=\"ChartCurve.Smooth\"` draws a curve through the values, which suits a measure that changes continuously between readings, such as temperature. The curve never overshoots a value, so it doesn't invent peaks the data doesn't have. Keep straight lines for counts and totals, where a curve suggests values between the points that were never measured." },
        { "id": "chart-line-step", "title": "Step lines", "lead": "`Curve=\"ChartCurve.Step\"` holds each value flat until the next one's date, then steps: for values that stay put until they change, such as a price, a rate or a plan's tier. Add a last item at today's date so the current value runs to the present." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "area-chart", "icon": "ChartArea", "name": "ChartAreaSeries", "title": "Area chart", "category": "Charts", "summary": "Shows a total over time as filled areas, stacked to show each part of the whole.", "keywords": ["area chart", "stacked area chart", "area graph"],
      "features": [
        { "feature": "Areas over dates, numbers or names, straight, smooth or stepped" },
        { "feature": "Stacked, or stacked to 100%" },
        { "feature": "Drawn on the server, as wide as its container, with labels that thin out as it narrows" },
        { "feature": "A table of the values in a \"Show data\" disclosure" },
        { "feature": "Tooltips on hover and from the keyboard", "noscript": "none" },
        { "feature": "Legend items that hide and show a series", "noscript": "none" }
      ],
      "examples": [
        { "id": "chart-area-channels", "title": "Parts of a total over time", "lead": "`ChartAreaSeries` fills the space under a line. Stacked with `Stacking`, the areas show a total over time and how it divides: the top edge is all visits, and each band one channel's part.", "after": ["Put the largest, steadiest part at the bottom, where its edge is straight and easiest to read.", "Everything on the Chart page works with every series type."] }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "scatter-chart", "icon": "ChartScatter", "name": "ChartScatterSeries", "title": "Scatter chart", "category": "Charts", "summary": "Shows how two measures relate, a marker for each item.", "keywords": ["scatter plot", "scatter chart", "xy chart", "correlation"],
      "features": [
        { "feature": "Two measures on number or date scales" },
        { "feature": "Groups split into series by a value, each with its own marker shape" },
        { "feature": "Drawn on the server, as wide as its container, with labels that thin out as it narrows" },
        { "feature": "A table of the values in a \"Show data\" disclosure" },
        { "feature": "Tooltips on hover and from the keyboard", "noscript": "none" },
        { "feature": "Legend items that hide and show a series", "noscript": "none" }
      ],
      "examples": [
        { "id": "chart-scatter", "title": "Load time against bounce rate", "lead": "To show how two measures relate across many items, use `ChartScatterSeries`, with one measure as the `Argument` and the other as the series' `Value`. Name both measures with each axis's `Title`, since nothing else says which is which.", "after": ["Each item is a marker, and both scales start where the values do rather than at zero.", "`SeriesBy` splits a series into one per value of a property, here the device, each with its own color and marker shape. `Trend=\"ChartTrend.Linear\"` draws each one's straight line of best fit.", "The tooltip is for the point nearest the pointer, and the arrow keys move between points from left to right. Here a `ChartTooltip` names the page."] }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "bubble-chart", "icon": "ChartScatter", "name": "ChartBubbleSeries", "title": "Bubble chart", "category": "Charts", "summary": "Shows three measures at once: a circle for each item at two, sized by the third.", "keywords": ["bubble chart", "bubble plot"],
      "features": [
        { "feature": "Circles sized by area, on one scale across series" },
        { "feature": "An optional size key of reference circles" },
        { "feature": "Each bubble named on the chart" },
        { "feature": "Drawn on the server, as wide as its container, with labels that thin out as it narrows" },
        { "feature": "A table of the values in a \"Show data\" disclosure" },
        { "feature": "Tooltips on hover and from the keyboard", "noscript": "none" },
        { "feature": "Legend items that hide and show a series", "noscript": "none" }
      ],
      "examples": [
        { "id": "chart-bubble", "title": "Named bubbles for a third measure", "lead": "A bubble chart is a scatter whose circles are sized by a third measure. Use `ChartBubbleSeries` and give it a `Size`, here each product's revenue.", "after": ["Circles are sized by area, on one scale across every series: revenue twice as large is a circle twice the area. The largest is capped to the plot. Larger bubbles are drawn first, so none hides a smaller one.", "`SizeFormat` sets how sizes read in the tooltip and in Show data. With few bubbles, name each one with `LabelText`; names that would collide are left out at the widths where they would."] },
        { "id": "chart-bubble-world", "title": "Many bubbles, by group, on a log scale", "lead": "To spread out values that span a hundredfold, such as income, set `Logarithmic` on `ChartArgumentAxis`: each step of ten is an equal distance apart. Here each country's income is plotted against how long its people live, sized by population and colored by region with `SeriesBy`.", "after": ["With this many bubbles, not every name fits. List the items largest first, and `LabelText` names the biggest, leaving out those that would collide.", "`ShowSizeKey` adds a key to the legend, with circles for a round size near the largest and a quarter of it, so a size can be read and not only compared. `SizeFormat` sets how sizes read there. Here the legend is beside the chart, with `Legend=\"ChartLegend.End\"`."] }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "waterfall-chart", "icon": "ChartColumnIncreasing", "name": "ChartWaterfallSeries", "title": "Waterfall chart", "category": "Charts", "summary": "Walks from a starting amount through rises and falls to a result, with totals along the way.", "keywords": ["waterfall chart", "bridge chart", "profit and loss chart", "cascade chart"],
      "features": [
        { "feature": "Rises, falls and totals in three colors, keyed in the legend" },
        { "feature": "Changes written signed, so color isn't the only cue" },
        { "feature": "Bars across for line items, columns for changes over time" },
        { "feature": "Drawn on the server, as wide as its container, with labels that thin out as it narrows" },
        { "feature": "A table of the values in a \"Show data\" disclosure" },
        { "feature": "Tooltips on hover and from the keyboard", "noscript": "none" }
      ],
      "examples": [
        { "id": "chart-waterfall", "title": "A profit and loss", "lead": "`ChartWaterfallSeries` draws each item's `Value` as a change from where the last bar ended, here from revenue down to net profit. Each item that `Total` marks stands on the baseline and shows the total so far, whatever its own `Value`: here gross, operating and net profit.", "after": ["The first bar, the starting amount, also stands on the baseline. Line items read best as bars across, with `Orientation` set to `Horizontal`, so their names stay flat.", "Rises are blue, falls red and totals gray, colors that stay apart for red-green color blindness. `IncreaseColor`, `DecreaseColor` and `Color` (the totals') set them.", "Labels write each change signed, such as −$498K. With every bar labeled, the value axis's labels and grid lines step aside. A waterfall is its chart's only series."] },
        { "id": "chart-waterfall-over-time", "title": "Changes over time", "lead": "For changes over time, such as a year's cash flow, keep the columns upright so time runs left to right: the opening balance, each month's net change, and the closing balance as a `Total`. The connectors show where each month starts." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "funnel-chart", "icon": "Funnel", "name": "FunnelChart", "title": "Funnel chart", "category": "Charts", "summary": "Shows the stages of a process that people drop out of, such as sign-ups, as a funnel, or levels of a hierarchy as a pyramid.", "keywords": ["funnel", "conversion", "pipeline", "drop-off", "pyramid", "sales funnel"],
      "features": [
        { "feature": "A funnel, each stage as wide as its value and sloping to the next" },
        { "feature": "A pyramid, each level as tall as its share of the total" },
        { "feature": "Each stage named beside the shape with its value and share, joined by a line, or in its stage where it fits" },
        { "feature": "Labels you write with a template" },
        { "feature": "A table of the values, with each stage's share of the one before, in a \"Show data\" disclosure" }
      ],
      "examples": [
        { "id": "funnel-chart-usage", "title": "A sign-up funnel", "lead": "A funnel chart shows the stages of a process that people drop out of, such as visits, sign-ups and purchases. Give `FunnelChart` the `Items` in the order of the process, each stage's name as `Argument`, its count as `Value`, and a `Title`.", "after": ["Each stage's top is as wide as its value, so the largest is the full width, and its sides slope to the next stage's width. Beside the funnel, a line joins each stage to its name, value and share of the first. \"Show data\" adds each stage's share of the one before."] },
        { "id": "funnel-chart-inside", "title": "Labels in the stages", "lead": "To write each stage's label in it, as its share over its name, set `Labels=\"ChartLabels.Inside\"`. A label that doesn't fit its stage, such as a narrow last stage's, sits beside the shape with a line instead.", "after": ["`LabelTemplate` replaces the labels with your own, inside or beside, built from each stage's `Name`, `ValueText`, `Share` and `ShareOfPrevious`. `ChartLabels.None` leaves the labels out."] },
        { "id": "funnel-chart-pyramid", "title": "A pyramid", "lead": "A pyramid shows levels of a hierarchy, such as a team by seniority. To draw one, set `Pyramid`: the first item is at the bottom, and each level is as tall as its share of the total, which its label shows.", "after": ["The wide base gives the first level more area than its share. To compare levels exactly, use a `Chart` with bars across."] }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "heatmap-chart", "icon": "Grid3x3", "name": "HeatmapChart", "title": "Heatmap", "category": "Charts", "summary": "Shows values over two dimensions, such as orders by weekday and hour, as a grid of cells whose color grows with the value.", "keywords": ["heatmap", "heat map", "matrix", "grid", "activity", "punch card", "color scale"], "parts": ["HeatmapRange", "HeatmapTooltip"],
      "features": [
        { "feature": "Cells colored in even steps from round bounds, light to dark, or blended continuously" },
        { "feature": "A diverging scale for values above and below a midpoint" },
        { "feature": "Named ranges with your own colors" },
        { "feature": "Values in the cells where they fit, in ink that reads on the shade" },
        { "feature": "Names along the bottom that thin out as the grid narrows, by month for dates" },
        { "feature": "A legend that is the color scale, beside or above the grid" },
        { "feature": "Legend keys that hide and show the cells in their class", "noscript": "none" },
        { "feature": "A table of the values, rows by columns, in a \"Show data\" disclosure" },
        { "feature": "Tooltips on hover and from the keyboard, cell by cell", "noscript": "none" }
      ],
      "examples": [
        { "id": "heatmap-chart-usage", "title": "Orders by weekday and hour", "lead": "A heatmap shows values over two dimensions as a grid, so a pattern across both stands out, such as a shop's busiest hours. Give `HeatmapChart` the `Items`, each one's `Column`, `Row` and `Value`, and a `Title`.", "after": ["Columns and rows keep the order the items first name them in, the first row at the top. `ColumnFormat` formats the column names, here hours as \"8 AM\".", "The values fall into about five classes between round bounds, here six of 10 orders each, shaded light to dark. The legend above the grid is that scale.", "The values show in the cells while every one fits. At narrower widths they're left out, and pointing at a cell shows its tooltip instead."],
          "notes": { "noscript": "The legend keys don't hide cells, and narrow grids have no tooltips. \"Show data\" lists every value." } },
        { "id": "heatmap-chart-diverging", "title": "Above and below normal", "lead": "For values on either side of a meaningful middle, such as each month's temperature against the normal, set `Palette` to `ChartPalette.Diverging` with a color for each side. The scale turns gray at `Midpoint` (0 by default).", "after": ["The scale reaches as far on both sides, so colors of the same strength mean the same distance from the middle. `Steps` sets about how many classes there are (5 by default); here the bounds round to four classes of 2°C, two on each side.", "`Legend=\"ChartLegend.End\"` stands the scale upright beside the grid, with the high end at the top."] },
        { "id": "heatmap-chart-ranges", "title": "Named levels", "lead": "When the values have levels with names of their own, such as a support team's \"Quiet\" to \"Overloaded\", give each a `HeatmapRange` with its `From`, `To`, `Color` and `Label`. The legend names each level with its bounds.", "after": ["Order the colors light to dark, with `light-dark()` for dark mode, where the strongest shade is the lightest. With IzzyBlazor's script, each legend key hides and shows the cells at its level."] },
        { "id": "heatmap-chart-year", "title": "A year, hour by hour", "lead": "A heatmap holds thousands of cells: here a site's visitors in each hour of each day of a year, 365 columns by 24 rows. `Steps=\"0\"` blends the color continuously, with a gradient for the legend.", "after": ["Along the bottom, the columns are named by month. When a row is shorter than a line of text (`CellHeight`), every second row name shows.", "Cells narrower than 6px lose their gaps and corners, so the grid reads as one surface. The cells are too small for values, so point at one for its tooltip."] }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "calendar-heatmap", "icon": "Grid3x2", "name": "CalendarHeatmap", "title": "Calendar heatmap", "category": "Charts", "summary": "Shows a value for each day, such as commits or orders, as a calendar of colored cells: a column per week, a row per weekday.", "keywords": ["calendar heatmap", "contribution graph", "activity calendar", "github calendar", "daily heatmap", "streak"], "parts": ["HeatmapRange", "HeatmapTooltip"],
      "features": [
